findings.body:
`assigned_variable_selector` is a pydantic `Sequence[str]`, so it arrives as a `list`. The new override tests `assigned_selector in variable_selectors`, where `variable_selectors` is a `set[tuple[str, ...]]`. Set membership hashes the key first, so this raises `TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'` even when the set is empty.

The coordinator calls this hook from `_build_paths_map` during `GraphEngine.start`, for every edge source on a root-to-response path that is not a branch, container or response node. So any v1 Variable Assigner upstream of an Answer node now crashes at startup, not only the case the PR targets. The v2 node avoids this by converting each selector with `tuple(...)` before comparing.

::diff{file="api/core/workflow/nodes/variable_assigner/v1/node.py" lines="36-43"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The new integration test uses this exact shape (a v1 assigner between Start and Answer), so it should fail with this TypeError. Please run it to confirm before relying on the test result.

::suggestion{file="api/core/workflow/nodes/variable_assigner/v1/node.py" lines="42-43"}
        assigned_selector = tuple(self.node_data.assigned_variable_selector)
        return assigned_selector in variable_selectors

The PR changes `inputs.value` for `WriteMode.CLEAR` from the (unused) input value to the zeroed variable value. The title and description cover only the streaming fix, and no test covers this change. Any consumer of the node's recorded inputs, such as run history or trace views, will see different data for clear operations after this change.

::diff{file="api/core/workflow/nodes/variable_assigner/v1/node.py" lines="102-111"}

If this behaviour change is intended, split it into its own PR with a test. If not, revert it. Either way, a CLEAR-mode assertion belongs in the tests.

The only test for the new behaviour is the end-to-end streaming test. It asserts on the final answer and on streamed chunks, but it does not call `blocks_variable_output` directly, and it does not check the negative case where the assigner writes a different variable. A direct unit test on the node (list selector versus tuple set, match and non-match, and the non-conversation case) would have exposed the TypeError immediately.

The new fixture also omits `version` on the assigner, so it silently relies on the factory default of '1'. Setting `version: '1'` explicitly would make the intent of the fixture obvious.

::code{file="api/tests/unit_tests/core/workflow/graph_engine/test_streaming_conversation_variables.py" lines="50-77"}

The v1 node imports `Set` and `Tuple` from `typing`, but the base node and the v2 node use the built-in generics `set[tuple[str, ...]]`. Matching the existing style keeps the three overrides consistent and removes two imports.

::diff{file="api/core/workflow/nodes/variable_assigner/v1/node.py" lines="2-2"}

